---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = "Stargazing on Mauna Kea: What to Know Before You Go";
const description = "Tips for stargazing on Mauna Kea, Hawaii's highest peak. Best times to visit, what to bring, altitude sickness prevention, and tour options from Hilo.";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="blog-post">
    <header class="post-header">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="category">Activities</span>
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <p class="excerpt">Tips for experiencing world-class stargazing at 13,800 feet, from the best times to visit to what to bring.</p>
        <time datetime="2025-01-01">January 1, 2025</time>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="post-content">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="lead">
          Mauna Kea is considered one of the best places on Earth for astronomical observation. At 13,803 feet, the summit rises above 40% of Earth's atmosphere and most of its water vapor, providing crystal-clear views of the night sky. The world's largest telescopes are here for a reason.
        </p>

        <h2>Why Mauna Kea?</h2>
        <p>
          Several factors make Mauna Kea exceptional for stargazing:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Altitude:</strong> Above most atmospheric interference</li>
          <li><strong>Low humidity:</strong> Clear, dry air above the cloud layer</li>
          <li><strong>Minimal light pollution:</strong> Hawaii has strict lighting laws</li>
          <li><strong>Stable atmosphere:</strong> Laminar airflow from surrounding ocean</li>
          <li><strong>Location:</strong> Access to both northern and southern sky objects</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Visitor Center Experience</h2>
        <p>
          The Maunakea Visitor Information Station (VIS) sits at 9,200 feet elevation—high enough for excellent stargazing but low enough to avoid the most severe altitude effects.
        </p>

        <h3>Free Stargazing Program</h3>
        <p>
          Every evening (weather permitting), the Visitor Center offers free stargazing from 6-10 PM. Rangers set up telescopes and guide visitors through the night sky. No reservation needed, but arrive by 6 PM for the best experience.
        </p>
        <p>
          You'll see:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Planets (Saturn's rings are spectacular through a telescope)</li>
          <li>Star clusters and nebulae</li>
          <li>The Milky Way (incredibly vivid from this altitude)</li>
          <li>Galaxies millions of light-years away</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Summit Visits</h2>
        <p>
          The summit at 13,803 feet offers even more spectacular views, but comes with additional challenges and restrictions.
        </p>

        <h3>Requirements</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>4WD vehicle required (road is steep and unpaved)</li>
          <li>No children under 13</li>
          <li>Not recommended for pregnant women or those with heart/respiratory conditions</li>
          <li>Must stop at Visitor Center for 30+ minutes to acclimatize</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Sunset at the Summit</h3>
        <p>
          Watching sunset from above the clouds is an otherworldly experience. The summit clears by 30 minutes after sunset, but the Visitor Center stargazing program continues until 10 PM.
        </p>

        <h2>Altitude Sickness Prevention</h2>
        <p>
          At nearly 14,000 feet, altitude sickness is a real concern. Follow these guidelines:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Acclimatize:</strong> Stop at the Visitor Center for at least 30 minutes before ascending</li>
          <li><strong>Hydrate:</strong> Drink plenty of water before and during your visit</li>
          <li><strong>No scuba diving:</strong> Wait 24 hours after diving before going to altitude</li>
          <li><strong>Know the symptoms:</strong> Headache, nausea, shortness of breath, dizziness</li>
          <li><strong>Descend if needed:</strong> If symptoms worsen, go back down immediately</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What to Bring</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Warm layers:</strong> Temperatures can drop below freezing, even in summer</li>
          <li><strong>Winter jacket, hat, gloves:</strong> Yes, in Hawaii!</li>
          <li><strong>Closed-toe shoes:</strong> Required at the summit</li>
          <li><strong>Water and snacks:</strong> None available at the summit</li>
          <li><strong>Full tank of gas:</strong> No services on the mountain</li>
          <li><strong>Flashlight with red filter:</strong> Preserves night vision</li>
          <li><strong>Camera with tripod:</strong> For astrophotography</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Best Times to Visit</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>For stargazing:</strong> New moon periods offer the darkest skies. Check the lunar calendar when planning your trip.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Weather:</strong> The summit is above the clouds 90% of the time, but check the Mauna Kea Weather Center for current conditions.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Seasons:</strong> Winter offers longer nights and views of the Orion Nebula. Summer provides views of the Milky Way core.
        </p>

        <h2>Tour Options</h2>
        <p>
          If you don't have a 4WD or prefer not to drive the challenging road, several tour companies offer sunset and stargazing tours from Hilo and Kona. They provide warm clothing, hot drinks, and expert guides.
        </p>

        <h2>Getting There from Island Goodes</h2>
        <p>
          The drive from Island Goodes to the Visitor Center takes about 1 hour via Saddle Road (Highway 200). The summit is another 30-45 minutes beyond the Visitor Center.
        </p>
        <p>
          For sunset viewing, leave Island Goodes 2-3 hours before sunset to allow time for acclimatization.
        </p>

        <div class="cta-box">
          <h3>Plan Your Stargazing Adventure</h3>
          <p>After an evening under the stars, return to the comfort of your private room at Island Goodes. Our location and peaceful atmosphere make the perfect recovery from your high-altitude adventure.</p>
          <a href="https://v2.reservationkey.com/3655/reserve/c" class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Book Your Stay</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
